# On the Existence of Excitonic Signatures in the Optical Response of Metal-Organic Frameworks: Comment on “van der Waals Metal-Organic Framework as an Excitonic Material for Advanced Photonics”

## abstract
In a recent experimental paper, it was claimed that pronounced excitonic signatures are observed in optical response of Zn-based metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) at room temperature. Performing ab initio modelling, it is demonstrated that an alternative interpretation based on single-electron optical transitions between narrow -bands in the system of aromatic rings of the ligand is far more plausible. Although these results do not rule the possibility of exciton formation in MOFs out completely, they show that extreme caution should be taken in attributing the features in photoabsorption spectra alone to excitons, and additional proof, such as data on long-distance energy transfer, is necessary.

In a recent experimental paper, it was claimed that pronounced excitonic signatures are observed in optical response of Zn-based metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) at room temperature. Performing ab initio modelling, it is demonstrated that an alternative interpretation based on single-electron optical transitions between narrow $\pmb{\pi}$ -bands in the system of aromatic rings of the ligand is far more plausible. Although these results do not rule the possibility of exciton formation in MOFs out completely, they show that extreme caution should be taken in attributing the features in photoabsorption spectra alone to excitons, and additional proof, such as data on long-distance energy transfer, is necessary.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are microporous compounds consisting of metal ions or clusters connected by organic linkers (ligands), and packed into 1D, 2D, or 3D structures.[1] It was recently proposed that one of the interesting features of MOFs is that they can be used as efficient lightharvesting substances in excitonic solar cells. The investigation of possible excitonic signatures in MOFs has been the subject of both experimental and theoretical investigation during last several years with the main focus being on their role in longdistance energy transfer.[2–5]
In the recent report,[6] it was suggested that one particular type of MOF, namely the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based van der Waals metal–organic framework $[\{\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{TBAPy})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\}^{\ast}3.5\mathrm{DEF}]_{n},$ where TBAPy $\mathbf{\delta}=\mathbf{\delta}1,3,6,8.$ -tetrakis(p-benzoate)pyrene and DEF is a diethyl­ formamide (MOF-Zn below), reveals strong signatures of excitonic response at room temperatures. The quantities under experimental study were absorption, reflection, and photo­ luminescence spectra. All of them revealed a pronounced peak at the absorption edge, which was fitted by superposition of two
Lorentzians attributed by the authors to inter- and intralayer excitons. The first one corresponds to the bound electron–hole pairs for which quasiparticles of the opposite charge are situated in the neighbor 2D layers, while the second one corresponds to the situation when an electron and a hole placed in the same layer forming a bound state. It was claimed that proposed classification of the peaks follows from the polarization dependence of the measured spectra.
In light of the experimental findings and their interpretation proposed in by Milichko et al.[6] our Comment here has several goals. First, as we will demonstrate below, the data presented in by Milichko et al.[6] can be persuasively explained as resulting from single-particle excitations only, without any necessity for the formation of excitons. The claim of Milichko et al.[6] that they “demonstrated for the first time the prospect of metal–organic frameworks as excitonic materials for simultaneous all-optical data processing and storage” thus remains not supported. In the broader perspective of the physics of van der Waals MOFs, we clearly demonstrate the importance of single-electron transitions between narrow $\pi$ -bands in the system of aromatic rings of the ligands, which, in the spectra of the optical response of the system, lead to the onset of the peaks’ near absorption edge. They may look very similar to those appearing due to excitonic effects, but have a completely different physical origin. This conclusion, besides being interesting from the point of view of fundamental physics, is also important for prospective practical applications based on excitonic storage in MOFs.
The main property of an exciton is that it represents twoparticle excitation, for which a positively charged hole and a negatively charged electron are bound together. This distinguishes excitons from single-particle excitations that can be also created in valence- to conduction-band optical transitions. As excitons have nonzero binding energy, their excitation becomes possible if the photon energy is below the bandgap; therefore, peaks in the optical response near the absorption edge can be often interpreted as excitonic. However, in complex metal–organic systems, this is not the only possible interpretation, and resonant features in absorption and luminescence can be explained in terms of single-particle excitations involving ligand-centered emission,[7] metal-centered emission,[8] ligand-to-metal charge transfer,[9] metal-to-ligand charge transfer,[10] interligand chargetransfer transitions,[11] and others.
In our work, we tested what kind of excitations, single particle or excitonic, are responsible for the reported experimental features Milichko et al.[6] We computed numerically the energetic spectrum and optical-response function of the MOF-Zn structure consisting of 2D metal–organic sheets connected with each other by weak van der Waals interactions, in agreement with the geometry of Milichko et al.[6] The elementary cell of the structure is shown in Figure 1. Our analysis includes three main computational steps.
The first one is coarse tuning and stability testing for the experimental X-ray diffraction (XRD) data of MOF- $Z\mathrm{n}^{\left[12\right]}$ with a simple semi-empirical approach based on the PM7 method[13] for the relaxation of the stressed system and determination of the equilibrium configuration using the MOPAC package.[14] Numerous optimizations of distorted equilibrium geometries show that the considered system is stable, which confirms the reliability of the XRD reconstruction.
The second step is ab initio calculation of spatial and electronic structures of the MOF-Zn within the frameworks of the density functional theory (DFT) with the exchange-correlation functional of Perdew, Burke, and Ernzerhof (PBE)[15] and the use of the Quantum ESPRESSO package.[16] As a result, the density of electronic states (DOS) of MOF-Zn was obtained, together with a projected density of states (PDOS) for the ligand and Zn nodes separately (Figure 2). The energy was rescaled so that the energy of the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) equals zero. In the context of our study, the most prominent feature of the calculated DOS is the presence of the two narrow bands near the edges of the conduction and the valence bands of the system. Comparing the total DOS with the PDOSs of Zn and ligand allows the conclusion to be made that these bands are related to the $\pi$ -orbitals of the system of the aromatic rings of the ligand. This conclusion is supported by the existing data on other types of aromatic compounds.[17,18] Optical transitions between them, corresponding to the range of photon energies of about $2.6{-}2.7~\mathrm{eV},$ can lead to the appearance of the peak-like structure near the absorption edge. To understand better the transition indicated by the horizontal black arrow in Figure 2, we plotted in Figure 3, the real-space profiles of the HOMO and lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO), in the plane of the one layer of the MOF-Zn calculated with the use of the exchange-correlation functional PBE[15] and the Quantum ESPRESSO package.[16] The strong localization of the HOMO and LUMO orbitals charge density in the aromatic rings area can be clearly seen.
Note, that DFT is a single-particle approximation and thus cannot, in principle, describe any excitonic effects, for which more advanced treatment based on Bethe–Salpeter equation is necessary. Therefore, if the transition discussed above is not prohibited by the selection rules, it will allow an interpretation of the observed resonant effects in the optical response based on single-particle transitions between the HOMO and LUMO of the ligand to be drawn, which has certain advantages compared to the excitonic mechanism (see below).
The last third step of computation is the calculation of the dipole strength function of the MOF-Zn system within the time-dependent density functional theory (TDDFT) using the exchange-correlation functional PBE[15] and the Quantum ESPRESSO package.[16] Note that the dipole strength function is proportional to the imaginary components of the polarizability tensor and thus accurately describes the shape of the photoabsorption spectrum. The main result of the calculation is shown in Figure 4. One clearly sees that a single-particle transition between the narrow $\pi$ bands discussed above are indeed possible and lead to the formation of the peak for photon energies of about $2.6{-}2.7~\mathrm{eV},$ in full agreement with the experimental data of Milichko et al.[6]
We stress once more that the origin of this peak is fully single particle and has nothing to do with excitonic effects. The conclusion that excitons are indeed not involved in its formation is supported by several important discrepancies between the excitonic scenario proposed by Milichko et al.[6] and their reported experimental data. Indeed, the binding energy of the putative interlayer exciton was claimed to be bigger than one for intralayer excitons, which contradicts the physical sense (indeed, if an electron and hole are placed in different layers, the distance between them becomes bigger and thus the electron–hole interaction is attenuated). Moreover, the reported dependence of the peak formation on the intensity of the pump, namely the tendency that the peak becomes more pronounced as the intensity increases, rules out the excitonic scenario, for which the opposite trend should be realized. Indeed, the increase of the pump should lead to the increase of the density of the electrons and the holes in the system, which makes the effects of the screening stronger and results in exciton dissociation and a gradual transition to the regime of unbounded electron–hole plasma (known as excitonic Mott transition in the literature). Our conclusions are also confirmed by the results of another recent study on similar MOF systems.[19]
Finally, we would like to stress that our analysis does not rule out the possibility of the exciton formation in MOFs completely, which was convincingly demonstrated in a series of previous reports.[2–5] However, it shows that some features of the optical response of MOFs that can seem, at first glance, excitonic, can be, in fact, attributed to single-particle excitations. If electron–hole Coulomb attraction would be accounted for, additional excitonic peaks would possibly appear below the bandgap energy. To prove the presence of the excitons in the MOF systems experimentally, one needs additional supportive data, such as evidence of long-distance energy transfer.
